##Import Data
I am changing my section a bit here from mike's because I pre-entered the data into my csv by hand from the data that came off of the skalar. In his code, he does it from R and exports the data to a csv.

I am keeping the part where he has the detection limit being changed. He explained how that comes from the skalar screenshots we take so if you have something that is below that detection limit, it is given a single value. this analysis needs to have a value so ignoring them is not as ideal.

He also mentioned how the time series might not truly be relevant because I did not do a reflooding experiment and it is hard to do a time series with one incubation because it has the assumption that the first 24 hours the cores are the most disturbed and odd things could be happening. The 48 hours should be the most stable and understandable, but then the last 72 hours would be a bottle neck effect and there isn't that realistic of data going on. The time series is really just the concentration on the y and the time point on the x if I was interested in exploring it.


```{r}
library(tidyverse)
library(forcats) #deciding order of bars
library(patchwork) #stacking plot panels
